  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> "We need to produce a merged description of the entity \"Anger\". The input includes a list of JSON objects, each with a \"Description\" field. The descriptions are:\\n\\n1. \"Anger is an emotional state that can be portrayed as a destructive force that outlasts transient political events, threatening personal relationships. It is also described as a suffering state resulting from feeling less important or gifted than others.\"\\n\\n2. \"Anger is an emotional state that leads to negative consequences such as health issues, strained relationships, and self-harm, often through blaming others or oneself. In the context of human behavior, anger is perceived as either a weakness or a catalyst for action, and is analyzed in relation to its suppression and consequences.\"\\n\\n3. \"Additionally, some descriptions note that anger can imprint negative energy on food during cooking.\"\\n\\n4. \"Anger is connected to problems related to itself, implying a cyclical relationship between emotional states and consequences.\"\\n\\n5. (duplicate of 4)\\n\\n6. (duplicate of 4)\\n\\n7. \"Anger is an intense emotion that can disrupt relationships and contribute to disconnection.\"\\n\\nWe need to integrate all key information. The summary must be objective, third-person, mention the full name of the entity at the beginning. Also, we need to handle conflicts: there are multiple descriptions of \"Anger is connected to problems related to itself, implying a cyclical relationship between emotional states and consequences.\" That's repeated. Also, there is a description about negative energy on food. Also, there is a description about being a destructive force that outlasts political events, threatening relationships, and being a suffering state from feeling less important. Also, negative consequences: health issues, strained relationships, self-harm, blaming others or oneself. Also, perception as weakness or catalyst, analysis of suppression and consequences. Also, intense emotion that can disrupt relationships and cause disconnection.\\n\\nWe need to produce a cohesive summary, multiple paragraphs, not exceeding 600 tokens. Must be plain text, no extra formatting.\\n\\nWe need to ensure we include all key points: emotional state, destructive nature, outlasting political events, threatening relationships, suffering from feeling less important, negative consequences (health issues, strained relationships, self-harm), blaming others or oneself, perception as weakness or catalyst, analysis of suppression and consequences, cyclical relationship: anger is connected to problems related to itself, implying a cyclical relationship between emotional states and consequences, negative energy on food during cooking, intense emotion disrupting relationships and causing disconnection.\\n\\nWe need to mention the entity name at the beginning: \\\"Anger is an emotional state...\\\"\\n\\nWe need to be objective, third-person.\\n\\nWe need to handle conflicts: The cyclical relationship description appears multiple times, but it's consistent. There's no conflict. The negative energy on food is a unique point. The description about being a destructive force that outlasts political events is also unique. The description about being a suffering state from feeling less important is also unique.\\n\\nWe need to integrate all.\\n\\nWe need to keep within 600 tokens. Let's count roughly: each token is roughly a word. We need to be concise.\\n\\nWe can structure:\\n\\nParagraph 1: Define anger as an emotional state, its destructive nature, outlasting political events, threatening relationships, and suffering from feeling less important.\\n\\nParagraph 2: Discuss negative consequences: health issues, strained relationships, self-harm, blaming others or oneself. Also, perception as weakness or catalyst, analysis of suppression and consequences.\\n\\nParagraph 3: Mention cyclical relationship: anger is connected to problems related to itself, implying a cyclical relationship between emotional states and consequences.\\n\\nParagraph 4: Note the belief that anger can imprint negative energy on food during cooking.\\n\\nParagraph 5: Summarize that anger is an intense emotion that can disrupt relationships and contribute to disconnection.\\n\\nWe need to ensure we don't exceed 600 tokens. Let's approximate: each paragraph maybe 100-120 tokens. 5 paragraphs => 600 tokens. So okay.\\n\\nWe need to avoid extraneous comments.\\n\\nNow, produce the output.\\n\\nWe need to ensure we mention the entity name at the beginning: \\\"Anger is an emotional state...\\\"\\n\\nWe need to be objective, third-person.\\n\\nWe need to include all key points.\\n\\nNow, write the summary.\\n\\nWe need to ensure we don't include any JSON or formatting.\\n\\nWe need to output plain text.\\n\\nLet's draft:\\n\\n\\\"Anger is an emotional state that can be portrayed as a destructive force, capable of outlasting transient political events and threatening personal relationships. It is also described as a suffering state arising from feeling less important or gifted than others. Anger can lead to negative consequences such as health issues, strained relationships, and self-harm, often through blaming others or oneself. In human behavior, anger is perceived as either a weakness or a catalyst for action, and is analyzed in relation to its suppression and consequences. Additionally, some descriptions note that anger can imprint negative energy on food during cooking. Anger is connected to problems related to itself, implying a cyclical relationship between emotional states and consequences. Finally, anger is an intense emotion that can disrupt relationships and contribute to disconnection.\\\"\\n\\nNow, check token count: Let's count roughly.\\n\\n\\\"Anger is an emotional state that can be portrayed as a destructive force, capable of outlasting transient political events and threatening personal relationships. It is also described as a suffering state arising from feeling less important or gifted than others. Anger can lead to negative consequences such as health issues, strained relationships, and self-harm, often through blaming others or oneself. In human behavior, anger is perceived as either a weakness or a catalyst for action, and is analyzed in relation to its suppression and consequences. Additionally, some descriptions note that anger can imprint negative energy on food during cooking. Anger is connected to problems related to itself, implying a cyclical relationship between emotional states and consequences. Finally, anger is an intense emotion that can disrupt relationships and contribute to disconnection.\\\"\\n\\nWe need to count tokens. Let's approximate:\\n\\n1. Anger (1)\\n2. is (2)\\n3. an (3)\\n4. emotional (4)\\n5. state (5)\\n6. that (6)\\n7. can (7)\\n8. be (8)\\n9. portrayed (9)\\n10. as (10)\\n11. a (11)\\n12. destructive (12)\\n13. force, (13)\\n14. capable (14)\\n15. of (15)\\n16. outlasting (16)\\n17. transient (17)\\n18. political (18)\\n19. events (19)\\n20. and (20)\\n21. threatening (21)\\n22. personal (22)\\n23. relationships. (23)\\n24. It (24)\\n25. is (25)\\n26. also (26)\\n27. described (27)\\n28. as (28)\\n29. a (29)\\n30. suffering (30)\\n31. state (31)\\n32. arising (32)\\n33. from (33)\\n34. feeling (34)\\n35. less (35)\\n36. important (36)\\n37. or (37)\\n38. gifted (38)\\n39. than (39)\\n40. others. (40)\\n41. Anger (41)\\n42. can (42)\\n43. lead (43)\\n44. to (44)\\n45. negative (45)\\n46. consequences (46)\\n47. such (47)\\n48. as (48)\\n49. health (49)\\n50. issues, (50)\\n51. strained (51)\\n52. relationships, (52)\\n53. and (53)\\n54. self-harm, (54)\\n55. often (55)\\n56. through (56)\\n57. blaming (57)\\n58. others (58)\\n59. or (59)\\n60. oneself. (60)\\n61. In (61)\\n62. human (62)\\n63. behavior, (63)\\n64. anger (64)\\n65. is (65)\\n66. perceived (66)\\n67. as (67)\\n68. either (68)\\n69. a (69)\\n70. weakness (70)\\n71. or (71)\\n72. a (72)\\n73. catalyst (73)\\n74. for (74)\\n75. action, (75)\\n76. and (76)\\n77. is (77)\\n78. analyzed (78)\\n79. in (79)\\n80. relation (80)\\n81. to (81)\\n82. its (82)\\n83. suppression (83)\\n84. and (84)\\n85. consequences. (85)\\n86. Additionally, (86)\\n87. some (87)\\n88. descriptions (88)\\n89. note (89)\\n90. that (90)\\n91. anger (91)\\n92. can (92)\\n93. imprint (93)\\n94. negative (94)\\n95. energy (95)\\n96. on (96)\\n97. food (97)\\n98. during (98)\\n99. cooking. (99)\\n100. Anger (100)\\n101. is (101)\\n102. connected (102)\\n103. to (103)\\n104. problems (104)\\n105. related (105)\\n106. to (106)\\n107. itself, (107)\\n108. implying (108)\\n109. a (109)\\n110. cyclical (110)\\n111. relationship (111)\\n112. between (112)\\n113. emotional (113)\\n114. states (114)\\n115. and (115)\\n116. consequences. (116)\\n117. Finally, (117)\\n118. anger (118)\\n119. is (119)\\n120. an (120)\\n121. intense (121)\\n122. emotion (122)\\n123. that (123)\\n124. can (124)\\n125. disrupt (125)\\n126. relationships (126)\\n127. and (127)\\n128. contribute (128)\\n129. to (129)\\n130. disconnection. (130)\\n\\nSo about 130 tokens. That's well under 600.\\n\\nBut we need to ensure we included all key points: we have:\\n\\n- destructive force, outlasting political events, threatening relationships, suffering from feeling less important.\\n\\n- negative consequences: health issues, strained relationships, self-harm, blaming others or oneself.\\n\\n- perception as weakness or catalyst, analysis of suppression and consequences.\\n\\n- cyclical relationship: anger is connected to problems related to itself, implying a cyclical relationship between emotional states and consequences.\\n\\n- negative energy on food during cooking.\\n\\n- intense emotion that can disrupt relationships and cause disconnection.\\n\\nWe also have the description about being a destructive force that outlasts political events, threatening personal relationships. That's included.\\n\\nWe also have the description a