<urn:mukthiguru:Childhood%20Ceremonies> a <neo4j://graph.schema#base>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """Childhood ceremonies are rituals performed during early development to shape samskaras. These ceremonies function as cultural or spiritual practices that influence the formation of an individual's character and values from a young age. The specific nature of these ceremonies can vary across different societies, but they share the common goal of instilling a sense of identity, belonging, and moral framework.

In many cultural contexts, particularly where the term samskaras is used, childhood ceremonies are believed to leave lasting imprints on a person's psyche, guiding behavior and thought patterns throughout life. The rituals may include symbolic actions, prayers, or communal gatherings, each designed to reinforce particular virtues or social roles. Despite variations, the underlying purpose remains the same: to nurture the development of the child's inner self and integrate them into the community.

Overall, childhood ceremonies serve as a mechanism for transmitting cultural values and spiritual concepts during formative years. They provide a structured way for children to learn about their heritage, develop self-awareness, and establish a foundation for future growth. The practice underscores the importance of early life experiences in shaping an individual's trajectory. The repeated short descriptions confirm that these ceremonies are indeed rituals performed during early development to shape samskaras.<SEP>Childhood ceremonies are rituals performed during early development to shape samskaras.` - Correct.<SEP>Childhood ceremonies are rituals performed during early development to shape samskaras.` - Correct.""";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#file_path> "unknown_source";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_id> "Childhood Ceremonies";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#created_at> "1779750360"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#long>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#truncate> "";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_type> "event";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#source_id> "chunk-9d8a8416ae7a083477cbb7ac15043bbb" .

