  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """<think>
Okay, let's tackle this. The user wants me to extract the actual spiritual content from the contaminated text provided. The contaminated text includes a lot of developer instructions and prompt templates mixed with the real content.

First, I need to identify the real spiritual teachings or concepts. The contaminated text mentions \"Chinese 'Chi' energy corresponds to Kundalini's life force and spiritual ascension\" and \"The Chinese are referenced as an ancient civilization with spiritual traditions.\" These are the key points.

The developer instructions talk about creating a summary, JSON objects, token counts, and formatting, which I need to ignore. The repeated entries of the same description about the Chinese civilization are redundant, so I should mention that once. The summary provided in the contaminated text seems to combine both aspects: the civilization's spiritual traditions and the concept of Chi related to Kundalini.

I need to make sure the output is a clean 1-4 sentence description in plain English without any bullet points or headers. Also, check if the entity name is mentioned at the beginning. The example output starts with \"Chinese refers to...\" so I should follow that structure.

Putting it all together: Start with the entity name, mention the ancient civilization and spiritual traditions, then explain Chi energy and its connection to Kundalini. Avoid any extra details or instructions. Make sure it's concise and within the token limit. Let me verify if there's any other spiritual content. The rest seems to be instructions or repetition, so the example summary provided in the contaminated text is the actual content. I can use that as a basis, trimming any unnecessary parts. The final answer should be the clean version of that summary.
</think>

Chinese refers to the people and civilization of China, an ancient culture with deep spiritual traditions. The Chinese have historically developed philosophical and religious systems that emphasize harmony, balance, and the flow of vital energy. In Chinese thought, 'Chi' (or Qi) is the life force that anim""";
