  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """<think>
Okay, let's tackle this. The user wants me to extract the actual spiritual teaching or concept definition from the contaminated text provided. The contaminated text includes a lot of developer instructions and repeated statements.

First, I need to identify the real content. The key part here is the definition of \"Consequences\" in the context of leadership. The repeated sentences mention that consequences are the outcomes of a leader's actions and policies, which can be positive or negative, immediate or delayed. They impact stakeholders, organizations, and society. The rest of the text seems to be instructions on how to structure the summary, like mentioning the entity name at the beginning and avoiding adding extra information.

I need to remove all the developer instructions and prompt templates. The actual definition is in the middle of the contaminated text. The important sentences are: \"Consequences, in the context of leadership, refer to the outcomes of a leader's actions and policies. Consequences can be immediate or delayed, and they may be beneficial or adverse, reflecting the impact of the leader's behavior on stakeholders, organizations, and broader society.\"

I should check if there's any other relevant content. The rest is repetition of the same definition and instructions. So the clean description should be those two sentences combined into a concise paragraph. Make sure it's in plain English, 1-4 sentences, and starts with the entity name. No bullet points or headers. Also, ensure that all developer notes are stripped out. The final answer should be just the definition without any extra text.
</think>

Consequences, in the context of leadership, refer to the outcomes of a leader's actions and policies. These can be immediate or delayed, and may be beneficial or adverse, reflecting the impact of leadership decisions on stakeholders, organizations, and society. Understanding consequences is essential for evaluating leadership effectiveness and guiding future policy development.""";
