  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """<think>
Okay, let's tackle this. The user wants me to extract the actual spiritual teaching or concept definition from the contaminated text provided. The contaminated text includes a lot of developer instructions and prompt templates mixed with the real content about \"Duty\".

First, I need to identify the real spiritual content. The contaminated text starts with instructions about producing a summary, integrating descriptions, handling conflicts, and structuring paragraphs. Then there's a draft paragraph that seems to be the actual content. The key here is to look for the part that defines \"Duty\" without the meta-instructions.

Looking at the draft paragraph: \"Duty is a concept that is presented in two complementary ways. In one view, it is described as a negative alternative to living in a state, characterized by heaviness and the inability to manage life effectively. This perspective also describes duty as a burdensome obligation, contrasted with living in a state. In another view, duty refers to an obligation-based approach to relationships and life, and is contrasted with the concept of state. Together, these descriptions portray duty as both a burdensome condition and a structured, obligation-based framework for living and relating to others.\"

This paragraph seems to be the actual content. The rest of the text is about how to structure the summary, token counts, and other instructions. The user wants only the clean description, so I need to extract this paragraph and ensure there's no extra formatting or instructions included. Also, check if there's any other relevant content. The contaminated text mentions the two themes but the draft paragraph already covers both aspects. The other parts are just instructions on how to create the summary, which should be removed. So the final answer should be the draft paragraph as is, without any headers or bullet points, just plain text.
</think>

Duty is a concept presented in two complementary ways. First, it is characterized as a negative alternative to living in a state, marked by heaviness, inability to manage life effectively, and burdensome""";
