<urn:mukthiguru:Freedom> a <neo4j://graph.schema#base>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_type> "concept";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#created_at> "1779737968"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#long>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#source_id> "chunk-d3d95285c3a8dfac657e743e02300c41<SEP>chunk-c3c756183e3f4bbcd6f10defea6c686f<SEP>chunk-6a2ca3b4a00fd65d72ac5932a227e2a8<SEP>chunk-6254a0998449b5aae9f36e202e6ddd4f<SEP>chunk-58209224da39691b43cec61c729a13d1<SEP>chunk-94adc3d4525b736bf33ffd5d5752ec38<SEP>chunk-339fa6ee789d0451cedd5e2548222d96<SEP>chunk-97418c51f26c4090e6f6b986b0a848a6<SEP>chunk-e2d0ff9dea6a5334fbc3569bce6e4413<SEP>chunk-922e23e61222fa7ebaae80af23f09b19<SEP>chunk-04a5cce20e9ca029b1d885a9ee7c4ec6<SEP>chunk-ecff7e1995c8cfd62a765753318d7dab<SEP>chunk-f7a7b6ec5216cd073aa9baeaf426fc77<SEP>chunk-e3a9d3b4e75a6781f9fb7a4d35559a14<SEP>chunk-f3cc07a478604197eefad4556e973256<SEP>chunk-07cef1cacdb43780b9533bba3a4b7ad2<SEP>chunk-e5a191d0b4c345edce809b4d1989b5be<SEP>chunk-0bcd34bbf2440bb034ae1a5607edb427";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#truncate> "";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#file_path> "The_Four_Sacred_Secrets.pdf<SEP>unknown_source<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B8wapoLAu84)<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8mmungGgDNw)<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O1VkNuEChD4)<SEP>YouTube Video: ajMAwlKh3YM (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajMAwlKh3YM)<SEP>YouTube Video: DmzZPgTh7_M (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DmzZPgTh7_M)<SEP>YouTube Video: cHAJiF2byzg (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cHAJiF2byzg)<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uGosHULQQk0)<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3weAVHuSCxg)";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_id> "Freedom";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """Freedom is a desired state of liberation from inner conflict, separation, and suffering, often linked to the concept of Beautiful Life. It is characterized by freedom from societal judgments and internal conflicts, achieved by moving beyond the constraints of unresolved desires and self‑imposed struggles. In this view, Freedom is the state of being free from anger and blame, attainable by letting go of negative thoughts and actions.  

Freedom is also presented as a paradoxical choice involving negative emotions, emphasizing liberation through self‑awareness. Some descriptions note that Freedom is a choice of negative emotions and is only for the wise, highlighting a nuanced perspective that not all can attain it. This paradoxical nature underscores the complexity of achieving true Freedom. Another facet of Freedom is its association with liberation from addiction and self‑imposed slavery, which is emphasized as the core of life's purpose. It is also described as the state of being free from anger and blame, achievable by letting go of negative thoughts and actions, tying Freedom to the broader goal of overcoming self‑imposed constraints. Freedom is closely linked to enlightenment, described as the absence of fear and a quiet mind amidst life's noise. This connection suggests that Freedom is not merely the absence of external constraints but also the cultivation of inner peace and clarity. Together, these perspectives portray Freedom as a multifaceted concept that integrates emotional, psychological, and spiritual dimensions.  

Freedom is described as a complex and debated concept, with references to its perceived absurdity in modern contexts. In this context, Freedom relates to autonomy over personal choices and lifestyle, emphasizing the individual's capacity to shape their own path. A debated concept in the video, analyzed for its perceived contradictions and societal implications, further highlights the contested nature of Freedom.  

Freedom is presented as a goal or state that individuals seek on social media platforms, but it is contrasted with the loss of privacy. Additionally, Freedom is presented as the ultimate aim of meditation, representing liberation and a state of being aligned with life's flow. This ultimate aim of meditation underscores Freedom as a spiritual objective, representing liberation and alignment with life's flow.  

Some of the provided descriptions are repeated, but the core ideas—liberation from inner conflict, the paradoxical nature of negative emotions, the link to enlightenment, and the various contexts (philosophical, meditative, social‑media, and video analysis)—are consistently reflected across the list.<SEP>Freedom is presented as a desired outcome, achieved through emotional release and acceptance.""" .

