  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """Guru Purnima is an ancient Hindu festival celebrated on the full moon day of the lunar month of Ashadha. It honors sage Vyasa and marks the completion of the Brahma‑Sutras, a seminal work in Hindu philosophy. The festival is a traditional Indian celebration that serves as a day of reverence for Vyasa, who is credited with compiling the Brahma‑Sutras. It is also observed as a day for seekers of enlightenment, celebrating the potential of human consciousness for enlightenment and liberation. The celebration emphasizes the importance of the sage's contributions and the spiritual significance of the full moon day in the lunar calendar.

The festival also serves as a celebration of enlightenment and the potential of human consciousness, a theme emphasized in Indian tradition. It is observed as a traditional day of celebration for seekers of enlightenment in India, specifically commemorating the completion of the Brahma‑Sutras by sage Vyasa. Additionally, the day is regarded as a celebration of the potential of human consciousness for enlightenment and liberation, highlighting the spiritual significance of the sage's contributions.<SEP>Guru Purnima is a traditional day of celebration for seekers of enlightenment in India, commemorating the completion of the Brahma-Sutras by sage Vyasa.""" .

