\"The hippocampus is a part of the brain involved in memory and learning. It is a structure located in the medial temporal lobe and plays a crucial role in the formation of new memories and learning processes. The hippocampus is part of the limbic system and is essential for spatial navigation and episodic memory.

Under conditions of stress, such as being judged, the hippocampus can shrink, which may affect memory. This shrinkage is thought to be mediated by stress hormones that can damage hippocampal neurons. The reduction in hippocampal volume has been linked to memory deficits, highlighting the importance of maintaining a healthy stress response for optimal cognitive function.

Overall, the hippocampus is a key brain region for memory and learning, and its vulnerability to stress underscores the interplay between emotional states and cognitive performance.\"

