<urn:mukthiguru:Killer%20Whales> a <neo4j://graph.schema#base>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#file_path> "YouTube Video: QzJ_Ft1de1o (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QzJ_Ft1de1o)<SEP>unknown_source";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#source_id> "chunk-5df138751f09bcc9c4d02f158a5b7e22<SEP>chunk-ba530f2475447367e3d5327e8faa29a8<SEP>chunk-b2b3f4e0ec4ca323c0c90b860c94bdb5<SEP>chunk-efef91f79f63a3dccc0da4a26bee1fef<SEP>chunk-f04ab7a9bf5f1c24c23dba56cff7596f";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#truncate> "";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """Killer whales are large marine mammals recognized for their high intelligence and exceptional hunting abilities. In scientific and domain-specific contexts, they are also referred to as 'orcas.'

As a species, killer whales exhibit a distinctive life history where females outlive males by a significant margin. This extended female lifespan, which extends into the post-reproductive stage, plays a crucial role in supporting offspring and grandchildren, thereby enhancing the survival and reproductive success of the species. The longer post-reproductive life of females contributes to the overall resilience of the population.<SEP>Killer whales are a species where females live significantly longer than males to support offspring and grandchildren.<SEP>Killer whales are a species where females live longer post-reproductive age to support younger generations, aiding species survival.<SEP>Killer whales are a species where females live significantly longer than males to support offspring and grandchildren.<SEP>Killer whales are a species where females live longer post-reproductive age to support younger generations, aiding species survival.<SEP>Killer whales are marine mammals known for their intelligence and social behavior.<SEP>Killer whales are marine mammals referred to as \"orcas\" in the text, with domain-specific terminology adjustments.""";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_id> "Killer Whales";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_type> "naturalobject";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#created_at> "1779656863"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#long> .

