  <neo4j://graph.schema#file_path> "The_Four_Sacred_Secrets.pdf<SEP>unknown_source<SEP>YouTube Video: HtmLQkxj8ec (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HtmLQkxj8ec)<SEP>YouTube Video: DmzZPgTh7_M (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DmzZPgTh7_M)<SEP>YouTube Video: Gdd-5uWUW5w (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gdd-5uWUW5w)<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U1qRvz3hnMA)";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """Poverty is a systemic factor that exacerbates challenges and affects individuals' ability to respond to adversity. While some descriptions emphasize material aspects, others highlight systemic and societal dimensions, these perspectives are complementary rather than contradictory. It is also described as a material challenge, characterized by a lack of financial resources, which further exacerbates life challenges. These perspectives highlight both the structural and economic dimensions of poverty.

Poverty is linked to broader societal issues, such as hunger‑driven rage and moral decay. It can also be viewed as a result of war's economic effects, indicating that conflict‑induced economic disruption contributes to the persistence of poverty. In some contexts, poverty is described as a state of economic hardship that may lead individuals to use anger as a means to overcome adversity.

Poverty is identified as a contributing factor to family struggles and caregiving responsibilities, specifically affecting Omit's responsibilities. This underscores how poverty can intensify familial burdens and limit caregiving capacity. Additionally, some descriptions suggest that anger might be employed to overcome economic hardship, reflecting a coping mechanism within the experience of poverty.

Spiritual teachings are noted to be adapted to diverse socioeconomic contexts, reflecting the varied ways in which poverty is understood and addressed across different cultural and religious frameworks. This adaptation highlights the multifaceted nature of poverty and the need for context‑sensitive approaches.<SEP>Poverty refers to the state of lacking sufficient resources for basic human needs, contributing to societal issues.`""";
