<urn:mukthiguru:Prana> a <neo4j://graph.schema#base>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_type> "concept";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#file_path> "unknown_source<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yDTGeNo-w0c)<SEP>YouTube Video: tegTDWT3SDc (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tegTDWT3SDc)<SEP>YouTube Video: ajMAwlKh3YM (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ajMAwlKh3YM)<SEP>YouTube Video: Lst4MvxPCx4 (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lst4MvxPCx4)<SEP>YouTube Video: cHAJiF2byzg (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cHAJiF2byzg)";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#truncate> "";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#source_id> "chunk-973b1f2ebeea7c21fa93382f4eedfdd4<SEP>chunk-daee63fc8106d8a10a17ec73a8e7fc6b<SEP>chunk-1685a3c1f2fe3dbf55dfa2a4eee6833c<SEP>chunk-aaeba0a2e3dac75ac3bf2d72063fa0eb<SEP>chunk-680dd7bb1c61258ea018e46f2177de41<SEP>chunk-e15affa7e924b9530baaf1cc0152bad4<SEP>chunk-3f9b4215b066d2d74445f8aa497138dc<SEP>chunk-7234398961838f3894f5b3cb8bdb630b<SEP>chunk-b32e50c1e5924b6f3ca08fb982fe71de<SEP>chunk-ecff7e1995c8cfd62a765753318d7dab<SEP>chunk-ccf8c615204507c39f3ca144b897ce7d";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#created_at> "1779662114"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#long>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """Prana is defined as the life force or vitality energy that underlies bodily functions and overall energy. It is also described as breath energy that governs physiological processes and vitality.

In yogic philosophy, Prana represents the vital energy present in the physical body. It is believed to enter the body during inhalation, where it cleanses and energizes cells. Through practices such as pranayama, Prana can be retained, influencing consciousness.

Beyond its physiological role, Prana is considered a mystical life force that can be manipulated during energy body work. It signifies not only vitality and energy levels but also the broader concept of life force in various spiritual contexts.<SEP>Ancient Sanskrit term for breath, considered the life force and sacred energy connecting all living beings.<SEP>Prana is the Sanskrit term for breath, representing life force and sacredness.<SEP>Prana refers to life force or spiritual energy, corrected from the misheard term \"Pyram.\"<SEP>A spiritual concept related to life force or energy, contrasted with physical limitations.<SEP>Domain-specific spiritual concept discussed in the video, misheard as \"Pyram\".""";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_id> "Prana" .

