  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> "Problems are challenges faced by both humans and tigers, as depicted in a documentary, and are also described as external challenges or obstacles that can be addressed without entering a state of suffering. These problems can accumulate in suffering states, creating a sense of entanglement and helplessness. From a philosophical perspective, problems are viewed as results of bad samsara and are addressed through inner work and consciousness. Additionally, health and society issues are framed as stemming from disconnection and stress, and challenges or issues manifesting in life are often linked to negative emotional states. Some descriptions also note that problems are global challenges and issues arising from... (the description is incomplete). The variety of perspectives suggests that problems can be understood both as external obstacles that can be solved without suffering and as internal, emotional, or spiritual conditions that require inner work and consciousness. The interplay between these views highlights the complexity of problems, encompassing personal, societal, and philosophical dimensions.<SEP>Problems are categorized into financial, relationship, and health issues, which are common human experiences.<SEP>Problems are associated with karma, described as negative outcomes resulting from consciousness and actions.<SEP>Problems are associated with karma, described as negative outcomes resulting from consciousness and actions.<SEP>Problems are associated with karma, described as negative outcomes resulting from consciousness and actions.<SEP>Problems are associated with karma, described as negative outcomes resulting from consciousness and actions.<SEP>Problems are associated with karma, described as negative outcomes resulting from consciousness and actions.";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#created_at> "1779667816"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#long>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_type> "concept" .

