<urn:mukthiguru:River> a <neo4j://graph.schema#base>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#created_at> "1779556342"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#long>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#source_id> "chunk-2c5321682aea240a40b41c720211188a<SEP>chunk-7f3bb991411cbbfa162f676b4fd10180<SEP>chunk-345cde2de9df7f9434b23cd5a304db8f<SEP>chunk-665f72ba5123be26210ce8f477e2012f<SEP>chunk-50a3afd25409a52a572d62a449dbc4ec<SEP>chunk-9ce1c80b4cf41412d36baabed73cd0a7<SEP>chunk-082ee41f700ea9a8a29b83bfbf36abec<SEP>chunk-0443cca2d76c87663467e3610969c2ba";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """<think>
Okay, let's start by identifying the actual spiritual content related to the River. The contaminated text includes several scenarios: the River as rising waters causing barriers, a fable where a young lion reflects on its identity, a woman crossing it with monks, a frog dismissing its significance, and Tulsidas swimming across. These elements symbolize challenges, self-discovery, and spiritual obstacles. I need to exclude the developer instructions about summarizing, token counts, and handling conflicts. The key is to present these scenarios concisely in plain English without bullet points or headers. Let me structure this into a coherent 1-4 sentence description.
</think>

The River serves as a symbolic barrier and transformative force, appearing in diverse contexts: it rises to create obstacles, reflects a young lion's identity crisis, challenges a woman crossing with monks, and is dismissed as illusion by a frog. Tulsidas encounters it as a spiritual trial, emphasizing its role in testing faith and self-perception.""";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#file_path> "The_Four_Sacred_Secrets.pdf<SEP>unknown_source<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M8XASiz30oE)";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#truncate> "";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_id> "River";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_type> "location" .

