<urn:mukthiguru:Sadness> a <neo4j://graph.schema#base>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#file_path> "YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B8wapoLAu84)<SEP>unknown_source<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fj2_d2lNGVk)<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M7ItOHTrvz8)<SEP>YouTube Video: ffhDTzE4nDU (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ffhDTzE4nDU)<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3weAVHuSCxg)";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#created_at> "1779737968"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#long>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """Sadness is an emotion that is included in the practice's observational framework and is mentioned in the text as part of emotional recognition. It is a negative emotional state characterized by sorrow or unhappiness.  

Sadness can become a persistent baseline mood if underlying states are not transformed. It can also transform into fear if unaddressed, as highlighted in the text.  

Sadness is an emotional state that children may experience, which parents can recognize. It is another emotional response linked to economic problems, categorized as an internal experience.  

Sadness is an emotional state that leaves impressions on clothes and skin, requiring cleansing rituals like turmeric water or salt.<SEP>An emotional state that affects the quality and impact of prepared food.<SEP>An emotional state that affects the quality and impact of prepared food.<SEP>Sadness is an emotional state of sorrow or unhappiness, often linked to loss or unfulfilled desires.<SEP>Sadness is a fundamental emotion linked to loss and pain, mentioned as a suffering state post-separation.""";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_id> "Sadness";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#truncate> "";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_type> "concept";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#source_id> "chunk-dd06aca0e8b0e45b68bcb022e995fab4<SEP>chunk-2946fd6f690a4f147698b0825594b6df<SEP>chunk-c29e446278bb16bd97c026c06682add6<SEP>chunk-5d0f74c4239eabf7a6fea87da28be5d8<SEP>chunk-6537d9939653cd5b7d036dced5f9de7b<SEP>chunk-1e483a5aae3e722cbcbaae7e0a2f3d19<SEP>chunk-9b20e4da8cf63963d1d2795ec8336f99<SEP>chunk-e32359164f62f64b8a0367195694bf0f<SEP>chunk-cd2b7f6d90a6185f05822cdaaff1892f<SEP>chunk-295ece324351da0c7194c4e2d4f58e99<SEP>chunk-801a54bafa1f63f0a4d091331cafc3d9<SEP>chunk-0bcd34bbf2440bb034ae1a5607edb427" .

<urn:mukthiguru:Sadness%20and%20Anger> a <neo4j://graph.schema#base>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_id> "Sadness and Anger";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#truncate> "";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """Sadness and Anger are valid emotions tied to experiences of injustice and division among groups. According to multiple descriptions, these emotions are recognized as legitimate responses to perceived injustice and social division. They reflect the emotional impact of unfair treatment and the fragmentation of group cohesion. The relationship between sadness and anger is often linked to the sense of loss and the desire for justice. In contexts where groups experience division, both emotions may arise, indicating the psychological toll of inequitable treatment and social fragmentation.

The coexistence of sadness and anger in response to injustice highlights the complex emotional landscape of group dynamics. Sadness may arise from the loss or suffering experienced, while anger may stem from the perceived violation of fairness. Together, they signal the need for reconciliation and equitable treatment within groups.<SEP>Sadness and anger are valid emotions tied to experiences of injustice and division among groups.<SEP>Sadness and anger are valid emotions tied to experiences of injustice and division among groups.<SEP>Sadness and anger are valid emotions tied to experiences of injustice and division among groups.<SEP>Sadness and anger are valid emotions tied to experiences of injustice and division among groups.<SEP>Sadness and anger are valid emotions tied to experiences of injustice and division among groups.<SEP>Sadness and anger are valid emotions tied to experiences of injustice and division among groups.""";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#created_at> "1779536296"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#long>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#file_path> "unknown_source";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_type> "concept";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#source_id> "chunk-122b26a4cc30222007d37133d11cda63" .

