<urn:mukthiguru:Separation> a <neo4j://graph.schema#base>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_id> "Separation";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#truncate> "";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_type> "concept";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#file_path> "The_Four_Sacred_Secrets.pdf<SEP>unknown_source<SEP>YouTube Video: pTnZt0SqDFM (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pTnZt0SqDFM)<SEP>YouTube Video: QAnRefsK-3Y (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QAnRefsK-3Y)<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1kS_mQaBLdg)<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jy4hcpUSBms)";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#created_at> "1779675421"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#long>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#source_id> "chunk-b96d370a14bedf82917d4f135e00825e<SEP>chunk-9126b7168266feeb5c044ddb65ae0392<SEP>chunk-46f092e49beb29848499bc5cec4e1faf<SEP>chunk-aaa5c02ef780f417e1e3498aff83c60b<SEP>chunk-8b68736730809289d016abf18cb33a9d<SEP>chunk-21d71f186fdeb1dfcf5eb219529f54f0<SEP>chunk-77a2fc8481ea11ffad7cb8ddb5fb08aa<SEP>chunk-1a7e64bb8a0d59d10204b5dfcccfe785<SEP>chunk-7a50476fe3717f3e56d4d65eb5669d3f<SEP>chunk-4f108a1313b1da3a2fa234ff5f49d6e5<SEP>chunk-c639141ec491d448e8e3d57ed9e03d3d<SEP>chunk-af881796b97d19f020179697b9daf675<SEP>chunk-08c5fbdcd35ed15300b9915ca1fced8a<SEP>chunk-1939ef89c77285b5d61536082f605bb4<SEP>chunk-607beefb79fd1c2b867abf7979a1dbfc<SEP>chunk-d333235fcabc3883b17563e023b36fd9<SEP>chunk-05cc795d538216c463ac6d6e2524a3ee";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """Separation is a concept that encompasses both a habitual state of self-centeredness and a disconnection that can be dissolved in a beautiful state of connection. It is often described as the opposite of oneness and is associated with negative emotions such as fear, pain, suffering, anger, hostility, violence, and prejudice. Some perspectives view separation as a consequence of suffering, leading to division among individuals and communities, while others identify it as the root cause of planetary disease and division. The sense of disconnection can persist even in social settings, hindering authentic relational depth and lasting love.

Separation can be addressed without suffering and is considered part of the inner state. It is a state of disconnection or division between individuals that dissolves when a beautiful state of connection is embraced. However, the habitual state of self-centeredness that characterizes separation is seen as a barrier to genuine relational depth and enduring love. Despite its negative associations, separation is also noted for its potential to be transformed through positive states.

The concept of separation is central to discussions of human experience, with some descriptions emphasizing its role as a cause of broader societal issues, while others highlight its impact on individual and community well-being. The duality of separation—as both a result and a cause of suffering—reflects its complex nature. It is also linked to the planet's disease, suggesting that addressing separation could have far-reaching benefits.<SEP>Separation refers to the perceived division from oneness or pure love, discussed as a central philosophical theme.<SEP>Separation denotes the act of creating divisions between groups based on differences, leading to exclusion or marginalization.<SEP>Separation refers to the perception of being distinct from others, which dissolves during conscious transformation.<SEP>Separation refers to the perception of being distinct from others, which dissolves during conscious transformation.<SEP>Separation refers to the perceived division between self and the universe, addressed through spiritual transcendence.""" .

