<urn:mukthiguru:Shrinking%20Self> a <neo4j://graph.schema#base>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """The Shrinking Self is a psychological construct that can be interpreted in two primary ways. One interpretation describes it as a state in which the limited sense of self dissolves, leading to a condition of undivided consciousness and a cessation of comparison with others. The other interpretation characterizes it as a maladaptive psychological state marked by persistent self-doubt, the need to compare oneself with others, and resulting feelings of inferiority, low self-esteem, and insignificance. These perspectives reflect both a transformative potential and a chronic, negative pattern of self-evaluation.

In the maladaptive view, the shrinking self is fueled by self-comparison, which drives self-consciousness, insecurity, and a feeling of being insignificant. It often leads individuals to derive self-worth from external validation, resulting in chronic dissatisfaction and a perception of life as an endless race. The state may also manifest as avoidance of life’s joys, as individuals become preoccupied with measuring themselves against others. Some descriptions further note that this condition can cause self-consciousness, comparison, and feelings of insignificance, and may be exemplified by specific behavior patterns, such as those observed in a person named Alex.

The concept is also linked to the notion of self-doubt and the need to compare oneself with others, which can produce feelings of inferiority. In some accounts, the shrinking self is described as a state where individuals derive self-worth from external validation and comparisons, leading to chronic dissatisfaction. Conversely, other descriptions emphasize the dissolution of a limited sense of self, resulting in undivided consciousness and a cessation of comparison.

Because the term is used across these varied contexts, it encompasses both a potentially beneficial transformation of self-perception and a persistent, negative pattern of self-evaluation. The descriptions vary in emphasis, with some focusing on the psychological mechanisms of comparison and low self-esteem, while others highlight the dissolution of the limited self and the cessation of comparison. The term remains a useful label for discussing the complex interplay between self-perception, social comparison, and emotional well-being.""";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#created_at> "1779184030"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#long>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_type> "concept";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#source_id> "chunk-670e795b68d71dbf734feac6e97cfa1f<SEP>chunk-063a5196900a330ccb49599d4025a638<SEP>chunk-13c1b39086ccfd163efe6f6f872777c0<SEP>chunk-0b07ef4dcf56fe79aaec78af52489ef5<SEP>chunk-da4b247a4eac03fe686b0059bd126928<SEP>chunk-676c070f41c08ddde395290878529cf3<SEP>chunk-747dd6f3fc344d596e672f021c5892ee<SEP>chunk-837d0412addc5b4967790622264e22cc<SEP>chunk-cb8bc4106969c883bf9c7f5bf8e466fc";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_id> "Shrinking Self";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#truncate> "";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#file_path> "The_Four_Sacred_Secrets.pdf<SEP>unknown_source" .

