  <neo4j://graph.schema#created_at> "1779665974"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#long>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#source_id> "chunk-b581d80fb3e1672b9184e7ccb9a394ea<SEP>chunk-312a7e6dcdab5fa1268941f1387c1ed0<SEP>chunk-cd8bf67cc8d8498084f884afbf901342<SEP>chunk-ed14675eaeee5b36e94642ad031a9dc4<SEP>chunk-e0651d1d759d9ec9887e923e28bd51f5<SEP>chunk-ade616c2567b32dc9ebbc4ced1ffc773";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> "Philosophical concept from Hindu traditions meaning \"That thou art,\" representing unity of self and universal consciousness.<SEP>Philosophical concept meaning \"That Thou Art,\" central to Hindu philosophy.<SEP>An enlightened state described as \"immeasurable\" where the sense of self dissolves into universal consciousness.<SEP>An enlightened state described as \"immeasurable\" where the sense of self dissolves into universal consciousness.<SEP>Tatvamasi refers to the enlightened consciousness state of \"I am\" as described in the text.<SEP>Tatvamasi is a Sanskrit term corrected from \"Tatwa Masih\" and \"Tatamasee\" in the text.<SEP>Tatvamasi is an enlightened state described as \"You are that,\" associated with spiritual unity and self-realization." .

<urn:mukthiguru:Tatvamasi/Tatparamasi> a <neo4j://graph.schema#base>;
