<urn:mukthiguru:Upasana> a <neo4j://graph.schema#base>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#created_at> "1779260126"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#long>;
  <neo4j://graph.schema#source_id> "chunk-398e965cd8ff044e9710f53404b93961<SEP>chunk-0ee4f7a7d24a0bfc0c26b19b67d4b249<SEP>chunk-a348f3e70c609d0d7798352cd61a2781<SEP>chunk-35b63d2ee19735d06397a5b887beddb0<SEP>chunk-5a85e64decd4f2a6f6b8b1a83aae1399<SEP>chunk-5a811ca674072f39c34abb4651e492c5<SEP>chunk-c87bd389c5c5a0aa7ee00af0f32f42e6<SEP>chunk-8d66f25e14fdb745367662bcfd5df1bf";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#description> """<think>
Okay, let's tackle this. The user wants me to extract the actual spiritual teaching about \"Upasana\" from the contaminated text provided. The contaminated text includes a lot of developer instructions and prompt templates mixed in with the real content.

First, I need to identify the key points about Upasana. The descriptions mention it's a spiritual practice in Hindu tradition, specifically ritualistic worship of Devi Saraswati or the Mother Goddess. It's performed during Navratri or Dashera, especially the first three days. The practice involves meditation, mantra recitation, and visualizations. The purpose is to dissolve anger and greed and fill practitioners with peace and generosity.

I should check for conflicts. Some descriptions mention different aspects, like being part of text correction scope, but that seems like a developer note. The main spiritual content is consistent across the points. The conflicts mentioned in the text are about whether it's a practice or ritual, but they can be reconciled as part of the same tradition.

Now, I need to condense this into 1-4 sentences without any markdown or headers. Make sure to remove all the developer instructions and just present the clean spiritual teaching. Let me piece together the essential elements: entity name, tradition, associated deity, festivals, practices involved, and the purpose.
</think>

Upasana is a spiritual practice in Hindu tradition involving ritualistic worship of Devi Saraswati or the Mother Goddess, particularly during Navratri or Dashera. It includes meditation, mantra recitation, and visualizations, especially during the first three days of these festivals, aiming to dissolve negative emotions like anger and greed while cultivating peace and generosity. The practice is described as part of Hindu worship traditions and text correction scope.""";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_type> "concept";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#entity_id> "Upasana";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#truncate> "";
  <neo4j://graph.schema#file_path> "unknown_source<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=owXqW04b08o)<SEP>YouTube Video: Unknown (URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=207izZBbqVg)" .

